    "metadata": {
      "title": "Nordic Market Entry: Workforce-Scheduling Software for Hospital Groups",
      "author": "Corporate Strategy",
      "company": "Board of Directors",
      "date": "9 September 2026"
    }
  },
  "children": [
    {
      "name": "section",
      "children": [
        {
          "name": "block",
          "props": {
            "ref": "cover",
            "slots": {
              "title": "Nordic Market Entry: Workforce-Scheduling Software for Hospital Groups",
              "subtitle": "Business Case for a Stockholm Office — Board Assessment",
              "client": "Board of Directors",
              "date": "9 September 2026",
              "confidentiality": "Confidential — Board Use Only"
            }
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "section",
      "children": [
        {
          "name": "block",
          "props": {
            "ref": "running-head",
            "slots": {
              "confidentiality": "Confidential — Board Use",
              "date": "9 September 2026"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "name": "block",
          "props": {
            "ref": "section-opener",
            "slots": {
              "number": "01",
              "title": "Recommendation"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "name": "block",
          "props": {
            "ref": "key-takeaways",
            "slots": {
              "items": [
                "The EUR 340m Nordic market grows 9% a year, and neither incumbent offers a Finnish-language product.",
                "A Stockholm office costs EUR 2.1m over 18 months and pays back by month 26 in the base case.",
                "We recommend entry, conditional on Finnish-language readiness, fast local hiring and early reference wins in each country."
              ]
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "name": "paragraph",
          "props": {
            "text": "We recommend proceeding with entry into the Nordic market through a Stockholm office in the next fiscal year. The region combines scale, growth and a language gap the two incumbents have left open, and the base-case economics repay the investment within the current planning horizon."
          }
        },
        {
          "name": "paragraph",
          "props": {
            "text": "Waiting increases the cost of entry: at 9% annual growth, a one-year delay costs the equivalent of roughly EUR 30 million of unclaimed market by the time a delayed office reaches selling capacity."
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "section",
      "children": [
        {
          "name": "block",
          "props": {
            "ref": "section-opener",
            "slots": {
              "number": "02",
              "title": "Market Opportunity"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "name": "paragraph",
          "props": {
            "text": "The addressable market across Sweden, Norway, Denmark and Finland is worth EUR 340 million and is growing at 9% a year, a pace that compounds the cost of waiting. Two incumbents hold 61% of the market between them, leaving a fragmented remainder of smaller and regional vendors. Neither incumbent offers a Finnish-language product, leaving Finland's hospital groups underserved by the two largest suppliers and open to a vendor that ships one at launch."
          }
        },
        {
          "name": "block",
          "props": {
            "ref": "callout",
            "slots": {
              "label": "Method note",
              "text": "Market size figures are illustrative estimates for board planning purposes and should be refined against a full addressable-market study before budget is finalized."
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "name": "paragraph",
          "props": {
            "text": "The remaining 39% of the market is split across smaller regional vendors with limited language coverage and thin implementation capacity, none of which combines Nordic-wide reach with the specialist product Finland's hospital groups need."
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "section",
      "children": [
        {
          "name": "block",
          "props": {
            "ref": "section-opener",
            "slots": {
              "number": "03",
              "title": "Entry Economics and Payback"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "name": "paragraph",
          "props": {
            "text": "Entry through a Stockholm office is costed at EUR 2.1 million spread over eighteen months, covering local hiring, go-to-market and the first wave of implementation support. Under the base case, cumulative investment is repaid by month 26; under the pessimistic case, which assumes slower deal velocity and higher discounting to win reference accounts, payback extends to month 34. Both fall inside a normal multi-year planning horizon for a market entry of this size, and the gap between them is the range the board should hold management accountable to."
          }
        },
        {
          "name": "block",
          "props": {
            "ref": "data-table",
            "slots": {
              "title": "Payback under base and pessimistic cases",
              "labelHeader": "Metric",
              "labels": [
                "Investment (EURm)",
                "Payback (months)",
                "Delta vs base (mo)"
              ],
              "columns": [
                {
                  "header": "Base case",
                  "cells": [
                    "2.1",
                    "26.0",
                    "0.0"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "header": "Pessimistic",
                  "cells": [
                    "2.1",
                    "34.0",
                    "+8.0"
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "source": "Internal business case model, Corporate Strategy, September 2026"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "name": "block",
          "props": {
            "ref": "kpi-row",
            "slots": {
              "items": [
                {
                  "value": "340",
                  "label": "Nordic TAM",
                  "unit": "€m"
                },
                {
                  "value": "9",
                  "label": "Annual growth",
                  "unit": "%"
                },
                {
                  "value": "61",
                  "label": "Incumbent share",
                  "unit": "%"
                }
              ],
              "source": "Market sizing and competitor share, internal analysis, Sep 2026"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "name": "paragraph",
          "props": {
            "text": "The payback range is driven almost entirely by sales-cycle assumptions rather than by cost: the EUR 2.1 million investment is fixed in both cases, so the gap between month 26 and month 34 measures how quickly the new office reaches a repeatable deal cadence."
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "section",
      "children": [
        {
          "name": "block",
          "props": {
            "ref": "section-opener",
            "slots": {
              "number": "04",
              "title": "Conditions the Recommendation Depends On"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "name": "paragraph",
          "props": {
            "text": "The recommendation is not unconditional. It depends on three things holding: that a Finnish-language product is ready at or before launch, since the language gap is the entry wedge rather than a nice-to-have; that the Stockholm office can hire a local sales and implementation team within the first two quarters, since payback timing assumes the team is selling by month six; and that at least one reference hospital group in each of the four countries signs within the first year, since Nordic healthcare buyers move on peer references more than on vendor claims."
          }
        },
        {
          "name": "heading",
          "props": {
            "text": "Risks and Mitigations",
            "level": 2
          }
        },
        {
          "name": "paragraph",
          "props": {
            "text": "The principal risk is incumbent response: both competitors could accelerate a Finnish-language release once entry is announced, narrowing the window the wedge depends on. A second risk is procurement cycle length in public and regional hospital groups, which can run longer than the eighteen-month build-out and push payback toward the pessimistic case. Mitigations are to lead with Finland to bank the wedge before competitors can respond, and to size the first-year pipeline to the pessimistic case rather than the base case."
          }
        },
        {
          "name": "heading",
          "props": {
            "text": "Conclusion",
            "level": 2
          }
        },
        {
          "name": "paragraph",
          "props": {
            "text": "The market is large, growing and structurally open in exactly the country where the incumbents are weakest. Both payback cases fall within an acceptable window, and the conditions above are within management's control to deliver. We recommend the board approve entry."
          }
        },
        {
          "name": "block",
          "props": {
            "ref": "footnotes"
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
